[
    {
        "text": "The thing we're interested in here is public key cryptography, in which you use a pair of keys, a private key that you keep secret and a public key that you can share. Both keys are linked, so by using your private key, you can easily generate the corresponding public key. But this only goes one way. It's virtually impossible for someone to take a public key and reverse engineer it to get the private key. Therefore, there's no security risk to you sharing the public key. In fact, you need to share it to make transactions. Private key holder can write a message and digitally sign it using their private key. By comparing a public key with a signature, you can safely assume that the person who owns the private key has written the message.",
